1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,334
Somewhere in America,

2
00:00:02,334 --> 00:00:04,785
a robot company is planning a giant

3
00:00:04,785 --> 00:00:05,877
new factory,

4
00:00:05,877 --> 00:00:08,260
a place where robots build robots.

5
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:09,680
Meet Gus.

6
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:11,206
He's the factory scout,

7
00:00:11,206 --> 00:00:12,976
and his boss has given him a

8
00:00:12,976 --> 00:00:14,110
single job.

9
00:00:14,110 --> 00:00:15,712
Find the best spot on Earth to

10
00:00:15,712 --> 00:00:16,770
build it.

11
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:17,912
For decades,

12
00:00:17,912 --> 00:00:20,150
that question had an easy answer.

13
00:00:20,280 --> 00:00:22,210
Go where workers are cheap.

14
00:00:22,210 --> 00:00:24,577
But Gus's new factory barely needs

15
00:00:24,577 --> 00:00:25,490
workers.

16
00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:27,360
So the answer changes.

17
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:29,600
And a country that never looked like

18
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:31,960
the obvious pick suddenly does.

19
00:00:32,090 --> 00:00:34,026
Could the robot in your future be

20
00:00:34,026 --> 00:00:35,487
made in Brazil?

21
00:00:50,550 --> 00:00:53,600
Gus works for Boltwell Robotics.

22
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:55,050
His boss spins a big globe.

23
00:00:55,050 --> 00:00:57,926
These robots work all night,

24
00:00:57,926 --> 00:00:59,300
she says.

25
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:00,685
They never get tired,

26
00:01:00,685 --> 00:01:02,880
and they need very few people.

27
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:05,290
So stop looking for cheap workers.

28
00:01:05,290 --> 00:01:06,257
Look for cheap,

29
00:01:06,257 --> 00:01:07,540
clean power.

30
00:01:07,540 --> 00:01:09,322
Look for the metals robots are made

31
00:01:09,322 --> 00:01:10,040
of.

32
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:13,410
And look for lots of customers.

33
00:01:13,410 --> 00:01:14,180
Gus nods.

34
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:14,449
So,

35
00:01:14,449 --> 00:01:15,070
where?

36
00:01:15,070 --> 00:01:16,580
The boss smiles.

37
00:01:16,580 --> 00:01:18,315
That's your job.

38
00:01:18,550 --> 00:01:21,030
Let me draw what changes.

39
00:01:21,150 --> 00:01:23,500
Here's the bill for making a robot.

40
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:24,964
In an old factory,

41
00:01:24,964 --> 00:01:26,639
the heaviest part was wages,

42
00:01:26,639 --> 00:01:28,220
people on the line.

43
00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:30,476
In a factory full of robots,

44
00:01:30,476 --> 00:01:31,610
that part shrinks.

45
00:01:31,610 --> 00:01:32,950
So what's left?

46
00:01:32,950 --> 00:01:35,440
The power that runs the machines.

47
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:37,060
The metal they're made of.

48
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:39,320
The cost of moving things around.

49
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:40,811
And the cost of the money you

50
00:01:40,811 --> 00:01:42,530
borrow to build it all.

51
00:01:42,660 --> 00:01:45,379
Whoever makes those cheapest wins the next

52
00:01:45,379 --> 00:01:46,795
factory.

53
00:01:47,050 --> 00:01:49,590
Then Gus gets a video call.

54
00:01:49,590 --> 00:01:50,261
It's Bia,

55
00:01:50,261 --> 00:01:52,550
an engineer from Minas Gerais,

56
00:01:52,550 --> 00:01:55,000
a big mining state in Brazil.

57
00:01:55,130 --> 00:01:56,631
She's read about the project,

58
00:01:56,631 --> 00:01:58,340
and she wants that factory in her

59
00:01:58,340 --> 00:01:59,320
home state.

60
00:01:59,390 --> 00:02:00,130
Come and see,

61
00:02:00,130 --> 00:02:00,880
she says.

62
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:01,923
We have the power,

63
00:02:01,923 --> 00:02:03,730
the metals and the people.

64
00:02:03,730 --> 00:02:05,539
Gus grabs a little backpack for the

65
00:02:05,539 --> 00:02:06,200
trip.

66
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:07,440
Bia laughs.

67
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:08,510
Pack light.

68
00:02:08,510 --> 00:02:09,953
You'll see why.

69
00:02:14,417 --> 00:02:15,202
First stop,

70
00:02:15,202 --> 00:02:17,610
a giant dam on a wide river,

71
00:02:17,610 --> 00:02:20,316
with wind turbines on the hills behind

72
00:02:20,316 --> 00:02:21,187
it.

73
00:02:21,357 --> 00:02:22,211
In Brazil,

74
00:02:22,211 --> 00:02:24,620
almost nine out of every ten units

75
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:27,248
of electricity come from renewable

76
00:02:27,248 --> 00:02:27,883
sources,

77
00:02:27,883 --> 00:02:29,394
mostly falling water,

78
00:02:29,394 --> 00:02:31,527
plus fast-growing wind and sun.

79
00:02:31,587 --> 00:02:33,387
Gus is impressed.

80
00:02:33,507 --> 00:02:35,599
A robot factory could run on that

81
00:02:35,599 --> 00:02:36,827
day and night.

82
00:02:36,977 --> 00:02:39,460
We told Brazil's power story in Could

83
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:42,792
Cheap Electricity Become the New Oil?

84
00:02:43,050 --> 00:02:44,102
Second stop,

85
00:02:44,102 --> 00:02:46,810
the red hills of Minas Gerais.

86
00:02:46,810 --> 00:02:48,902
Huge trains roll out of the mines,

87
00:02:48,902 --> 00:02:50,524
loaded with iron ore,

88
00:02:50,524 --> 00:02:52,770
the rock that becomes steel.

89
00:02:52,990 --> 00:02:55,249
Brazil is the second-biggest producer of

90
00:02:55,249 --> 00:02:56,790
iron ore on Earth.

91
00:02:56,890 --> 00:02:58,682
Gus pulls up a robot arm on

92
00:02:58,682 --> 00:02:59,660
his phone.

93
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:02,331
And robots are mostly steel.

94
00:03:02,583 --> 00:03:05,290
Then Bia hands Gus a tiny jar

95
00:03:05,290 --> 00:03:07,243
of gray powder.

96
00:03:07,423 --> 00:03:09,293
This is niobium.

97
00:03:09,313 --> 00:03:11,153
A pinch of it makes steel much

98
00:03:11,153 --> 00:03:12,903
stronger and lighter.

99
00:03:13,043 --> 00:03:14,906
And Brazil mines more than nine out

100
00:03:14,906 --> 00:03:16,769
of every ten tons of it in

101
00:03:16,769 --> 00:03:17,783
the world.

102
00:03:18,023 --> 00:03:19,943
Gus nearly drops the jar.

103
00:03:19,943 --> 00:03:22,203
You have almost all of it?

104
00:03:22,263 --> 00:03:23,663
Bia grins.

105
00:03:23,663 --> 00:03:25,619
Most of it comes from a single

106
00:03:25,619 --> 00:03:27,088
mine in my state.

107
00:03:27,350 --> 00:03:29,482
Robots also need motors,

108
00:03:29,482 --> 00:03:31,910
and the best motors need magnets made

109
00:03:31,910 --> 00:03:33,320
from rare earths.

110
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:35,343
Brazil has the second-largest known

111
00:03:35,343 --> 00:03:37,745
reserves of rare earths on the planet,

112
00:03:37,745 --> 00:03:40,147
though today it barely mines them,

113
00:03:40,147 --> 00:03:41,910
and China still does most of that

114
00:03:41,910 --> 00:03:42,730
work.

115
00:03:42,820 --> 00:03:44,950
And in the Jequitinhonha Valley,

116
00:03:44,950 --> 00:03:47,295
lithium for batteries is already being dug

117
00:03:47,295 --> 00:03:49,067
up and shipped abroad.

118
00:03:49,317 --> 00:03:51,987
Let me draw the treasure map.

119
00:03:52,197 --> 00:03:53,787
Here's Brazil.

120
00:03:53,987 --> 00:03:56,487
Rivers and wind for power.

121
00:03:56,597 --> 00:03:58,357
Iron in the hills.

122
00:03:58,357 --> 00:04:01,627
Niobium in a single mining town.

123
00:04:01,717 --> 00:04:04,817
Rare earths and lithium underground.

124
00:04:04,907 --> 00:04:05,421
Farms,

125
00:04:05,421 --> 00:04:08,457
and big cities full of customers.

126
00:04:08,547 --> 00:04:09,579
On paper,

127
00:04:09,579 --> 00:04:12,225
that's almost the whole recipe for a

128
00:04:12,225 --> 00:04:12,992
robot,

129
00:04:12,992 --> 00:04:15,445
inside a single country.

130
00:04:15,683 --> 00:04:16,555
Next,

131
00:04:16,555 --> 00:04:17,953
the farms.

132
00:04:17,953 --> 00:04:20,720
Fields of soybeans roll to the horizon,

133
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:23,512
and Brazil is the world's biggest exporter

134
00:04:23,512 --> 00:04:24,873
of soybeans.

135
00:04:25,063 --> 00:04:28,213
Food and land are no problem here.

136
00:04:28,413 --> 00:04:30,933
Gus takes a photo for his boss.

137
00:04:30,973 --> 00:04:32,798
She texts back a whole row of

138
00:04:32,798 --> 00:04:34,538
exclamation marks.

139
00:04:34,783 --> 00:04:37,483
And there's something people outside

140
00:04:37,483 --> 00:04:39,703
Brazil often forget.

141
00:04:39,733 --> 00:04:41,668
It already knows how to build hard

142
00:04:41,668 --> 00:04:42,483
things.

143
00:04:42,513 --> 00:04:44,676
Brazilian companies make passenger jets

144
00:04:44,676 --> 00:04:46,230
that fly all over the world,

145
00:04:46,230 --> 00:04:48,393
and electric motors sold on every

146
00:04:48,393 --> 00:04:49,583
continent.

147
00:04:49,603 --> 00:04:51,748
Bia studied with engineers who work on

148
00:04:51,748 --> 00:04:52,563
them.

149
00:04:52,593 --> 00:04:54,322
We don't just dig things up,

150
00:04:54,322 --> 00:04:55,333
she says.

151
00:04:55,333 --> 00:04:57,358
We build things too.

152
00:04:57,617 --> 00:04:58,191
Last,

153
00:04:58,191 --> 00:04:59,577
the city.

154
00:04:59,577 --> 00:05:00,729
Busy streets,

155
00:05:00,729 --> 00:05:01,290
shops,

156
00:05:01,290 --> 00:05:02,146
traffic,

157
00:05:02,146 --> 00:05:03,337
music.

158
00:05:03,367 --> 00:05:05,429
More than two hundred million people live

159
00:05:05,429 --> 00:05:06,234
in Brazil,

160
00:05:06,234 --> 00:05:08,915
a huge market for everything a factory

161
00:05:08,915 --> 00:05:09,917
makes.

162
00:05:09,917 --> 00:05:12,037
Gus counts on his fingers.

163
00:05:12,037 --> 00:05:12,884
Power,

164
00:05:12,884 --> 00:05:13,730
metal,

165
00:05:13,730 --> 00:05:14,577
magnets,

166
00:05:14,577 --> 00:05:15,131
food,

167
00:05:15,131 --> 00:05:16,607
customers.

168
00:05:16,677 --> 00:05:18,334
So why isn't everyone building here

169
00:05:18,334 --> 00:05:19,487
already?

170
00:05:19,697 --> 00:05:21,177
Bia sighs.

171
00:05:21,177 --> 00:05:23,210
Let me show you the backpack.

172
00:05:27,650 --> 00:05:29,110
Here's the twist.

173
00:05:29,110 --> 00:05:29,890
For years,

174
00:05:29,890 --> 00:05:32,311
Brazil's factories have been losing

175
00:05:32,311 --> 00:05:33,060
ground.

176
00:05:33,060 --> 00:05:34,998
Today they make only about one-eighth of

177
00:05:34,998 --> 00:05:37,320
everything the country produces.

178
00:05:37,320 --> 00:05:39,842
Economists have a long word for it,

179
00:05:39,842 --> 00:05:41,830
deindustrialization.

180
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:43,560
Gus looks around.

181
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:45,260
With all this treasure?

182
00:05:45,260 --> 00:05:46,550
Bia nods.

183
00:05:46,550 --> 00:05:49,221
Making things here costs extra.

184
00:05:49,483 --> 00:05:52,060
The backpack tour begins at the tax

185
00:05:52,060 --> 00:05:53,233
counter.

186
00:05:53,313 --> 00:05:54,200
In Brazil,

187
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:57,073
the bill to produce carries extra weight.

188
00:05:57,343 --> 00:05:59,566
As a part moves from factory to

189
00:05:59,566 --> 00:06:00,434
factory,

190
00:06:00,434 --> 00:06:01,745
it picks up taxes,

191
00:06:01,745 --> 00:06:03,524
and some taxes land on top of

192
00:06:03,524 --> 00:06:04,614
other taxes,

193
00:06:04,614 --> 00:06:06,103
like a snowball.

194
00:06:06,453 --> 00:06:08,562
Even the power bill carries a heavy

195
00:06:08,562 --> 00:06:08,926
load,

196
00:06:08,926 --> 00:06:10,268
as we saw in the new oil

197
00:06:10,268 --> 00:06:11,443
episode.

198
00:06:11,813 --> 00:06:12,793
Thunk.

199
00:06:12,793 --> 00:06:15,938
The first brick lands in Gus's backpack.

200
00:06:16,183 --> 00:06:16,747
Next,

201
00:06:16,747 --> 00:06:18,533
the paperwork.

202
00:06:18,723 --> 00:06:21,178
Brazil's tax rules are famous for being

203
00:06:21,178 --> 00:06:22,023
thick.

204
00:06:22,043 --> 00:06:23,709
In the World Bank's last worldwide

205
00:06:23,709 --> 00:06:24,876
comparison,

206
00:06:24,876 --> 00:06:27,019
companies here spent more hours on tax

207
00:06:27,019 --> 00:06:29,383
forms than anywhere else on Earth.

208
00:06:29,383 --> 00:06:30,433
Thunk.

209
00:06:30,433 --> 00:06:31,743
Another brick.

210
00:06:31,833 --> 00:06:34,338
Gus leans a little to the left.

211
00:06:34,583 --> 00:06:35,372
Then,

212
00:06:35,372 --> 00:06:36,803
the road.

213
00:06:36,823 --> 00:06:38,499
Brazil is enormous,

214
00:06:38,499 --> 00:06:40,929
and about two of every three tons

215
00:06:40,929 --> 00:06:42,848
of its cargo ride on trucks,

216
00:06:42,848 --> 00:06:44,281
over long highways,

217
00:06:44,281 --> 00:06:45,853
to busy ports.

218
00:06:46,043 --> 00:06:47,764
Over distances this big,

219
00:06:47,764 --> 00:06:49,920
trains and ships are usually cheaper,

220
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:52,263
but there are far fewer of them.

221
00:06:52,303 --> 00:06:53,093
Thunk.

222
00:06:53,093 --> 00:06:54,933
Another brick.

223
00:06:54,933 --> 00:06:56,607
Gus starts to sweat.

224
00:06:56,850 --> 00:06:58,213
And finally,

225
00:06:58,213 --> 00:06:59,370
the bank.

226
00:06:59,680 --> 00:07:01,018
To build a factory,

227
00:07:01,018 --> 00:07:02,100
you borrow.

228
00:07:02,100 --> 00:07:04,500
And Brazil's central bank keeps its key

229
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:07,673
interest rate at almost fourteen percent,

230
00:07:07,673 --> 00:07:08,980
high on purpose,

231
00:07:08,980 --> 00:07:11,180
to keep prices in check.

232
00:07:11,330 --> 00:07:13,794
So every loan in the country costs

233
00:07:13,794 --> 00:07:14,262
more,

234
00:07:14,262 --> 00:07:17,080
for families and for factories.

235
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:18,250
Thunk.

236
00:07:18,250 --> 00:07:20,244
Gus's knees wobble.

237
00:07:20,483 --> 00:07:21,984
Add it all up,

238
00:07:21,984 --> 00:07:24,404
and the backpack has a name,

239
00:07:24,404 --> 00:07:26,211
the Custo Brasil,

240
00:07:26,211 --> 00:07:28,343
the cost of Brazil.

241
00:07:28,513 --> 00:07:30,787
A study by a business group,

242
00:07:30,787 --> 00:07:33,520
working with Brazil's industry ministry,

243
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:36,024
put the extra cost of producing here,

244
00:07:36,024 --> 00:07:38,757
compared with the average rich country,

245
00:07:38,757 --> 00:07:41,283
at about three hundred billion dollars a

246
00:07:41,283 --> 00:07:42,113
year.

247
00:07:42,433 --> 00:07:43,726
That's an estimate,

248
00:07:43,726 --> 00:07:45,203
but it's heavy.

249
00:07:45,463 --> 00:07:48,103
Gus sits down on his backpack.

250
00:07:48,103 --> 00:07:48,703
Or,

251
00:07:48,703 --> 00:07:50,880
it sits down on him.

252
00:07:51,117 --> 00:07:53,777
Now here's the catch for robots.

253
00:07:53,777 --> 00:07:55,117
Let me draw it.

254
00:07:55,217 --> 00:07:57,406
Robots take the wage brick out of

255
00:07:57,406 --> 00:07:58,827
the backpack.

256
00:07:58,987 --> 00:08:01,389
But they don't touch the tax brick,

257
00:08:01,389 --> 00:08:04,117
the truck brick or the loan brick.

258
00:08:04,317 --> 00:08:07,189
And a robot factory is mostly expensive

259
00:08:07,189 --> 00:08:08,051
machines,

260
00:08:08,051 --> 00:08:10,197
bought with borrowed money.

261
00:08:10,277 --> 00:08:12,521
So in a robot factory,

262
00:08:12,521 --> 00:08:14,261
the loan brick gets heavier,

263
00:08:14,261 --> 00:08:15,617
not lighter.

264
00:08:15,937 --> 00:08:18,190
Robots make Brazil's treasure more

265
00:08:18,190 --> 00:08:19,289
valuable,

266
00:08:19,289 --> 00:08:21,861
and its backpack more painful.

267
00:08:26,317 --> 00:08:27,829
The good news is,

268
00:08:27,829 --> 00:08:30,827
Brazil knows about the backpack.

269
00:08:30,827 --> 00:08:33,038
It recently passed the biggest change to

270
00:08:33,038 --> 00:08:35,447
its sales taxes in decades.

271
00:08:35,457 --> 00:08:37,454
A pile of old taxes is being

272
00:08:37,454 --> 00:08:39,652
replaced by a simpler system,

273
00:08:39,652 --> 00:08:41,649
built so that taxes stop landing on

274
00:08:41,649 --> 00:08:43,137
top of taxes.

275
00:08:43,197 --> 00:08:45,507
Bia pulls the first brick out.

276
00:08:45,507 --> 00:08:46,147
See?

277
00:08:46,147 --> 00:08:48,146
It's already a little lighter.

278
00:08:48,383 --> 00:08:49,646
The catch is,

279
00:08:49,646 --> 00:08:50,823
it's slow.

280
00:08:50,823 --> 00:08:52,801
The new system started its test run

281
00:08:52,801 --> 00:08:53,438
this year,

282
00:08:53,438 --> 00:08:55,855
and the old taxes don't fully disappear

283
00:08:55,855 --> 00:08:57,763
until twenty thirty-three.

284
00:08:57,763 --> 00:08:59,703
Gus checks his calendar.

285
00:08:59,723 --> 00:09:01,598
That's a long time to carry a

286
00:09:01,598 --> 00:09:02,667
backpack.

287
00:09:02,917 --> 00:09:05,437
And factories can come back.

288
00:09:05,607 --> 00:09:06,544
In Bahia,

289
00:09:06,544 --> 00:09:08,466
Ford closed its giant car plant in

290
00:09:08,466 --> 00:09:09,977
Camaçari and left.

291
00:09:09,977 --> 00:09:11,147
The halls went quiet.

292
00:09:11,187 --> 00:09:13,719
Then a Chinese carmaker,

293
00:09:13,719 --> 00:09:14,328
BYD,

294
00:09:14,328 --> 00:09:16,220
took over the site,

295
00:09:16,220 --> 00:09:19,425
and began building cars there in twenty

296
00:09:19,425 --> 00:09:20,987
